DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The hhaasshh3322() functions are used to give a consistent and general inter‐
     face to a decent hashing algorithm within the kernel.  These functions
     can be used to hash ASCII NUL terminated strings, as well as blocks of
     memory.

     A _l_e_n argument is the length of the buffer in bytes.  A _c_o_u_n_t argument is
     the length of the buffer in 32-bit words.

     The hhaasshh3322__bbuuff() function is used as a general buffer hashing function.
     The argument _b_u_f is used to pass in the location, and _l_e_n is the length
     of the buffer in bytes.  The argument _h_a_s_h is used to extend an existing
     hash, or is passed the initial value HASHINIT to start a new hash.

     The hhaasshh3322__ssttrr() function is used to hash a NUL terminated string passed
     in _b_u_f with initial hash value given in _h_a_s_h.

     The hhaasshh3322__ssttrrnn() function is like the hhaasshh3322__ssttrr() function, except it
     also takes a _l_e_n argument, which is the maximal length of the expected
     string.

     The hhaasshh3322__ssttrree() and hhaasshh3322__ssttrrnnee() functions are helper functions used
     by the kernel to hash pathname components.  These functions have the ad‐
     ditional termination condition of terminating when they find a character
     given by _e_n_d in the string to be hashed.  If the argument _e_p is not NULL,
     it is set to the point in the buffer at which the hash function termi‐
     nated hashing.

     The jjeennkkiinnss__hhaasshh() function has same semantics as the hhaasshh3322__bbuuff(), but
     provides more advanced hashing algorithm with better distribution.

     The jjeennkkiinnss__hhaasshh3322() uses same hashing algorithm as the jjeennkkiinnss__hhaasshh()
     function, but works only on _u_i_n_t_3_2___t sized arrays, thus is simpler and
     faster.  It accepts an array of _u_i_n_t_3_2___t values in its first argument and
     size of this array in the second argument.

     The mmuurrmmuurr33__3322__hhaasshh() and mmuurrmmuurr33__3322__hhaasshh3322() functions are similar to
     jjeennkkiinnss__hhaasshh() and jjeennkkiinnss__hhaasshh3322(), but implement the 32-bit version of
     MurmurHash3.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The hhaasshh3322() functions return a 32 bit hash value of the buffer or
     string.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
           LIST_HEAD(head, cache) *hashtbl = NULL;
           u_long mask = 0;

           void
           sample_init(void)
           {

                   hashtbl = hashinit(numwanted, type, flags, &mask);
           }

           void
           sample_use(char *str, int len)
           {
                   uint32_t hash;

                   hash = hash32_str(str, HASHINIT);
                   hash = hash32_buf(&len, sizeof(len), hash);
                   hashtbl[hash & mask] = len;
           }

